course: dictionary
lesson: 12429
title: Formal words: deceased
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Deceased is a formal word that means dead. You will mostly see it in letters, forms, news reports and official papers, not in everyday talk. «Her deceased grandfather loved to fish in the river.» The word tells you the grandfather has died.
explain: Deceased can also be a noun, the deceased, meaning the person who has died. «The family of the deceased sent flowers to the funeral.» Ask yourself: who has died? The answer is the deceased, not the family.
explain: Friends and families usually say "died" or "passed away" when they talk. Deceased is a careful, polite word for writing where people need to be clear and respectful. When you see it, look at the words around it to check who or what it describes.
fact: Deceased means dead, and it is a formal word.
fact: «Recently deceased» means someone who died not long ago.
fact: The deceased, as a noun, means the person, or people, who have died.
fact: In everyday talk, people usually say "died" or "passed away".
fact: Records in history lessons often list deceased people, such as the names of people who died in a war or a disaster.
exercise: «Her deceased grandfather loved to fish.» What does deceased mean here? | *Dead | Far away | Asleep | Very tired
exercise: «The lawyer read the will to the family of the deceased.» Who is the deceased? | *The person who has died | The house they lived in | Lawyers who read wills | The family
exercise: Which word is the formal way to say that someone has died? | *Deceased | Hungry | Happy | Silly
test: «The recently deceased man was a famous teacher.» What does recently deceased mean? | Has just been born | *Died not long ago | Is going on holiday | Lives next door
test: Which word is best for an official form about someone who has died? | Cheerful | *Deceased | Nearby | Yummy
exam: Nadia says deceased is too fancy and "died" is better. Why might a newspaper use deceased? | It means happy and cheerful | It is only used by children | It means the same as a shop | *It is a formal word that suits serious, respectful writing
